We may think that we can favour a reaction simply by increasing temperature. However, the dependence of Gibbs free energy on temperature is different for different Log$Q_c$ values. For this reason, in fact, we do not always favour spontaneity just by increasing the temperature. 
Let analyze an experiment in which we increase the temperature going from points \emph{a} to \emph{b} following the arrow in Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-2dGLines}.
For example, in Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-2dGLines} (Left panel), we have that the reaction is nonspontaneous in both \emph{a} and \emph{b} temperature conditions. The increasing of  temperature did not help spontaneity.  Differently, on Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-2dGLines} (Right panel), we have that the reaction is not spontaneous in in \emph{a} but becomes spontaneous in \emph{b}. That is, increasing the temperature did promote spontaneity. Hence, selecting the right value for Log$Q_c$, that is the right ratio between reactants and products is important. 

For very small Log$Q_c$  values, the isoenergy lines increase with temperature, curving upwards. This means that when the temperature increases, $\Delta G_R$ becomes more negative, hence favouring spontaneity. In this situation, the thermodynamic force that drive the reaction would become stronger. For large Log$Q_c$  values, the isolines decrease with temperature, curving downwards. This means that when the temperature increases, $\Delta G_R$ becomes less negative, hence disfavouring spontaneity. There is a crossing Log$Q_c$ value (Log$Q_c^*$) that separates isolines curving upwards and downwards given by
\begin{equation}
\boxed{  \text{Log}Q_c^*=\frac{\Delta S^{\circ}}{Ln10\cdot R}    }\label{\chapterlabel:equationCriticalQ}
\end{equation}
In short, Log$Q_c$ needs to be smaller than entropy so that temperature can promote a reaction. Only for Log$Q_c$ values smaller than $\text{Log}Q_c^*$, we can favour spontaneity by increasing temperature. For example, on Figure \ref{Fig:Figure-2dGLines} (Left panel) $\text{Log}Q_c^*$ is approximately -3, whereas on the right panel, approximately +3.
But, even when Log$Q_c$ is smaller than the critical value, the temperature has to be large enough for a reaction to proceed spontaneously. For low temperature, even when we do not cross $\text{Log}Q_c(critical)$, the reaction will not proceed spontaneously. The crossing temperature value is given by
\begin{equation}
\boxed{ T^*=\frac{\Delta H^{\circ}}{\Delta S^{\circ}-R\cdot Ln10\cdot\text{Log}Q_c}    }\label{\chapterlabel:equationCriticalT}
\end{equation}
The crossing values for $T$ and $Q_c$ are different by nature. By comparing Equations \label{\chapterlabel:equationCriticalQ} and \label{\chapterlabel:equationCriticalT} we could see that while the crossing temperature value depends on $\Delta H^{\circ}$, $\Delta S^{\circ}$ and $\text{Log}Q_c$, the crossing $Q_c$ value only depends on $\Delta S^{\circ}$.

The information presented here demonstrates how a combination of low enough pressure conditions and large enough temperatures is needed for a reaction to happen spontaneously. 
